 "_meta": {
  "what_this_is": "a deterministic sample of ACCEPTED mined records, read and judged one by one by a human. Threat model v2, M5: sampling only the residue cannot find a false POSITIVE, and TM2-F1 is that a false positive counts as classified AND recorded, so the residue ledger balances while a config line sits in the product under a citation. This file is the only measurement of the positive side.",
  "sample_rule": "sorted(record ids), random.Random(20260917).sample(ids, 60), then sorted. qa.py re-draws exactly this sample and fails if the drawn ids differ from the ids reviewed here -- a review of a sample nobody can redraw is an assertion, not a measurement.",
  "reviewer": "Milo Vance, Toolkit Systems Engineer",
  "reviewed_on": "2026-09-17",
  "method": "each command was read against its source family and judged on two questions. is_command: is this a runnable command invocation as the source presents it, rather than prose, output, or configuration file content. complete: is the text a whole command, rather than a line the vendor's PDF wrapped and cut mid-token or mid-quote.",
  "sample_size": 60,
  "seed": 20260917,
  "is_command_true": 60,
  "complete_true": 53,
  "finding": "60/60 are genuine command invocations. 7/60 are truncated by the vendor's own line wrapping; all seven are flagged `trunc` by looks_truncated() and are barred from evidence. One of the seven (n=8, an ansible line cut inside a single-quoted -a argument) was marked complete on the first read and the detector caught it -- recorded here rather than quietly corrected, because a review that only ever agrees with the code is not a review. The product must render a truncated record as visibly incomplete: it is still the real beginning of a real command and worth showing, but it is not runnable as printed.",
  "thresholds": {
   "min_is_command_rate": 0.95,
   "every_incomplete_must_carry_trunc": true
  }
 },
